% M7-T7. A SEARCH ARRAY THAT MIXES VARIABLES AND CONSTANTS -- the shape that D-0068
% measured as 143 of the 278 corpus refusals, more than every other cause combined.
%
% Provenance and honesty. This is a HAND-SHRUNK reconstruction of the MiniZinc Challenge
% roster/scheduling shape that hits this: four task starts that must be pairwise
% distinct and pairwise ordered, annotated with an `int_search` over the whole slot
% array -- where two slots were fixed by the data and so came out of the flattener as
% INTEGER LITERALS, not variables. The corpus lives on fataepyc-07 (D-0068) and is not
% reachable from this checkout, and the original instances declare domains far wider
% than CLAUDE.md's wide-domain rule permits in a test, so the family's shape was
% reproduced at width 5 rather than an instance copied. What it reproduces exactly is
% the thing under test: `int_search([t1, 3, t2, t3, 1, t4], ...)`.
%
% Before M7-T7 the front end refused this model outright, on the first literal, with
% "the search array must contain variables, not constants". It now skips the literals
% and searches [t1, t2, t3, t4] in the order the annotation wrote them, which is what
% docs/SPEC.md 3.4 means by honouring the annotation.
%
% input_order + indomain_min over [t1, t2, t3, t4] forces exactly one answer and it is
% the lexicographically least: t1 = 1, then t2 > t1 and distinct gives 2, then t3
% distinct from both gives 3, then t4 > t3 and distinct gives 4.
var 1..5: t1 :: output_var;
var 1..5: t2 :: output_var;
var 1..5: t3 :: output_var;
var 1..5: t4 :: output_var;
constraint all_different_int([t1, t2, t3, t4]);
constraint int_lin_le([1, -1], [t1, t2], -1);
constraint int_lin_le([1, -1], [t3, t4], -1);
solve :: int_search([t1, 3, t2, t3, 1, t4], input_order, indomain_min, complete) satisfy;
